@* Ported from AssignCashier.ascx + .ascx.cs + .ascx.designer.cs.

   REVIEW: behaviour inferred from the original control tree - verify against the intended
   POS feature. This control is DELIBERATELY content-free, and that reproduces the legacy
   rendering exactly:
     * AssignCashier.ascx declared ONE server control, <asp:PlaceHolder ID="ScrudPlaceholder"
       runat="server" />, and nothing else.
     * AssignCashier.ascx.designer.cs therefore declared the ScrudPlaceholder field.
     * AssignCashier.ascx.cs overrode OnControlLoad with an EMPTY body - it NEVER added a
       Scrud (or anything else) to that placeholder, unlike the CRM/Setup controls that use
       the same placeholder idiom.
   An unpopulated asp:PlaceHolder renders NO element and NO content, so the legacy screen was
   blank. Inventing a <Scrud .../> here would be a guess at an unknown key column, table and
   view, and would newly expose a POS table through a CRUD form - a behaviour change, not a
   port. The placeholder idiom is recorded here instead so the feature's owner can supply the
   real Scrud parameters. *@

@inherits MixERP.Net.Framework.Controls.MixERPUserControl

@code {

    /// <summary>
    ///     MixERPUserControlBase.OnControlLoad is abstract, so every ported control must declare
    ///     it. The legacy override had an empty body (verified against AssignCashier.ascx.cs):
    ///     the control's entire server-side behaviour is the base class's logging and the two
    ///     authorization checks, which MixERPUserControl runs.
    /// </summary>
    public override void OnControlLoad(object sender, EventArgs e)
    {
    }
}
